 ## Overview
-Data Slicing is an algorithm for the rapid selection and representation of scalar field data on regular grids, enabling fast interactive cycling through data space by selecting and rendering specific value intervals [S1]. It solves the problem of real-time visualization of large, complex 3D scalar fields by avoiding the computational cost of back-to-front sorting required by traditional volume rendering [S1]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a pre-sorted linear list of data values to filter points near a contour or interval, rendering them with a constant color which eliminates the need for depth sorting [S1].
+Data Slicing is an algorithm for the rapid selection and representation of scalar field data on regular grids, enabling fast interactive cycling through data space by selecting and rendering specific value intervals [S1]. It solves the problem of real-time visualization of large, complex 3D scalar fields by eliminating the computational cost of back-to-front sorting required by traditional volume rendering [S1]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a pre-sorted linear list of data values to filter points near a contour or interval, rendering them with a constant color which eliminates the need for depth sorting [S1].
 
